copied from a friend's post
```
Create table a (a int, b int);

Create table b (a int, b int);
Insert into a value (1,1);
Insert into a value (2,2);
Insert into b values (2,2);
Insert into b values (1,1);
------Even if the ON condition is always false, table a needs to output because the ON condition is used to select table b
Select * from a left join b on 1=0;
A B A B
-------------------
1 1
2 2
------If the ON condition only involves table a and is true, then there is no restriction on table b, and both rows of b will be output, equivalent to a Cartesian product
Select * from a left join b on a.a=<reviewer>;
A B A B
------------ -- ------------
1 1 2 2
1 1 1 1
2 2 2 2
2 2 1 1
------If the ON condition can connect to table b but there are additional predicates, then table b will output when the predicate is true, and only table a will output when the predicate is false
Select * from a left join b on a. a=b. a and a. b=2;
A B A B
------------ -- ------------
2 2 2 2
1 1
```
